  "scope": "Two independent author roots reproduce the complete WIT archive. Author and build roots are removed before offline installation. One independently compiled public C caller executes the actual Component Model host and its native Lean host before and after moving the installed tree, for two executions per selection; caller source and compilation tools are removed before execution and generated package sources remain installed. The full fixture checks per-site constructor selection and normalization over all nineteen primitive bases, fixed-width wrapping, signed zero, refused non-finite floats, Unicode scalars, embedded NUL text, binary bytes, repeated record fields, active variant branches, aliases, mixed Fin guards, exact rejection paths, host-level shape and encoding refusals, caller-input preservation and recovery after rejection. Independent controls replay the retained records with coordinated record, execution, tool, resource, environment, package and compiler-model mutations. Primitive-base nominal WIT acceptance covers the tested acyclic records, variants, aliases, nested Pair/Array/Option/Except values, mixed Fin guards and constructor-free results over Unit, Bool, UInt8, UInt16, UInt32, UInt64, Int8, Int16, Int32, Int64, Nat, Int, Float32, Float, String, ByteArray, Char, USize and ISize on Linux x64/glibc 2.36 with an explicit 2.36 package floor, Wasmtime 42.0.1 and wasm-tools 1.245.1, with 64-bit native machine words. Two independent author builds reproduce each archive. Author/build roots are removed before offline installation, and caller compilation inputs are removed before the identical executable runs and replays after relocation. Generated package sources remain installed. The Component Model adapter requires its native host and is not a standalone WASI command. Refined callbacks, owned, recursive, dependent and broader generic Subtypes remain separate work. Constructor/source entry counts and memory are unmeasured; local runs do not establish hosted 2.38 release-floor acceptance. The archive retains original text members, compiled components, caller binaries and complete producer and control receipts, but not complete native archives or native libraries. Compiler and operating system distributions remain trusted.",
